{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CfukjBsMJpE8DrWgz5Xbo","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"It first sends the standard MCP `initialize` JSON-RPC request. That request carries the client's supported `protocolVersion`, its `capabilities`, and its `clientInfo` (name and version). Once the server replies with its own capabilities and info, the client sends a `notifications/initialized` notification. Only after that does it start discovery calls like `tools/list`, `prompts/list`, and `resources/list`.\n\nAt the transport level, with streamable HTTP the `initialize` goes out as the first HTTP POST. With the legacy SSE transport, the client first opens the SSE stream with a GET and then posts `initialize`. If the server requires OAuth, the connection first hits a 401, so the auth flow comes before a successful handshake.\n\nThis comes from my knowledge of the MCP spec and the Agents SDK, which wraps the official MCP client. I haven't checked it against the SDK source here."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2249,"cache_read_input_tokens":535,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2249},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"22332c40-9c58-4734-9d70-c1a7c989f169","uuid":"be71a566-3753-4797-8b22-b99d736fd275","timestamp":"2026-10-11T03:39:50.166Z","request_id":"req_011CfukjBehaqZbFLTJbtUTr"}
